Found by the first native run of the Python hook contracts against the Perl CLI on Windows (CI run 44244359693, job "Installed distribution / windows / Perl 5.40"): tests/test_briefing.py test_user_skill_resolves_from_unicode_home fails; the CLI briefs "could not be loaded: user-skill" while the Python hook passes. Not a k7 regression: on main the Windows leg stopped at the Perl tests (t/05, t/40) before the Python contracts ran, so this contract never ran natively against the CLI before.

Likely cause (unverified on Windows): lib/App/Briefing.pm:74-85 reads HOME, USERPROFILE, HOMEDRIVE+HOMEPATH from %ENV; Perl on Windows sees the environment in the ANSI code page, so a profile path outside it arrives mangled and user-level lookups miss.

Impact once binaries are pinned (k7): Windows users whose profile path is outside the ANSI code page lose user-level skills; project skills are unaffected. Same family as k8 (argv).

Recommendation: read the home variables through the wide API in the encapsulated Windows filesystem layer, with this contract as the native proof; land it before the plugin pin commit that activates the binaries. Decision is Getty's: outside the approved k7 design. Until then the k7 branch marks the contract as a known failure on Windows with the Perl CLI, naming this card.

